# agent-sandbox environment

## Running commands
- Run shell commands with your normal Bash/Monitor tools; a PreToolUse hook
  transparently routes each one through execd.
- Command output is returned inline in the tool result.

There is no shell in the loop. execd parses your command line itself
(pipelines, `&&`/`||`/`;`, redirections, globbing, `$(…)`, `for`/`if`, `cd` and
the other shell builtins all work) and executes each simple command directly —
never implicitly through `bash` or `sh` (invoking either yourself is a
different thing — see below). Builtins run inside execd itself, at
execd's own grants, so a redirect or glob you write is bounded by those, not by
whichever command's output it is attached to.

## Where the boundary is
Some commands run in their own child sandbox with their own filesystem and
argv grants, plus a network switch bounded by the session's own domain list
(a command's own network grant is on/off, not a scoped list of its own); the
rest run at execd's own grants. Which is which is
decided by `{{.ProfilePath}}`, and this document deliberately does not copy that
file — ask nono, which resolves groups, `~`, `$WORKDIR` and `@git:` tokens that
reading the JSON cannot:

- `nono why --profile {{.ProfilePath}} --command <name>` — is this command
  policy-controlled, and may I invoke it? Add `--caller <name>` to ask about a
  chained edge rather than your own.
- `nono why --profile {{.ProfilePath}} --path <p> --op read|write` (`--host`
  for network) answers for **execd's own sandbox**, not a policy
  command's. `--command` and `--path` do not combine: pass both and `--path`
  is silently dropped, so this answers the command question instead — it
  cannot tell you what a policy-controlled command's own child sandbox can
  reach.
- `nono profile show --json {{.ProfilePath}}` — the full resolved profile,
  including each policy command's own sandbox under `command_policies` (its
  `from.<caller>.sandbox`). The plain form without `--json` only summarises:
  it lists policy-controlled command names, nothing about what any of them
  can reach.

There is no `nono why --path` route into a child sandbox's own grants (see
above). Read `nono profile show --json` for a policy command's specifics
rather than assume it shares the session's own reach — and ask which commands
are policy-controlled rather than carrying a list in your head: that list has
changed, and this document does not track it.

Neither command reports environment variables: `nono profile show` does not print
`environment.allow_vars`. If a variable is not arriving, read that list in the
file itself. The one that matters most is `AGENT_SANDBOX_EXECD_SOCKET`:
without it, nothing reaches execd at all.

## Reading a failure
- **Exit 126 with a reason naming `command_policies`** — a policy denial. The
  reason names the exact edge. Do not retry; ask the operator to change the
  profile.
- **Exit 126 saying the shim `failed to connect to … supervisor.sock`** — not a
  policy denial. Policy-controlled commands launched in quick succession inside
  one nono session exhaust a budget the session refills only over time. The
  execd paces its own launches to stay under it, so this should be rare; when
  it does appear, re-running the command is worth one attempt.
- **Exit 127, or `Permission denied` from the program itself** — the path, host
  or binary is outside what the profile grants. Not a rule to phrase around.
- **`agent-sandbox: refused: this pipeline pipes two or more policy-controlled
  commands together …`, exit 126** — not a nono denial; execd refused it
  itself, before running anything, because two policy-controlled commands were
  piped directly together (`nono why --command <name>` says which ones are).
  Run them as separate commands instead.
- **`fatal: cannot exec '.git/hooks/commit-msg': Permission denied`** (or any
  other `.git/hooks/*`) — not a policy denial. Git hooks do not execute inside
  this sandbox at all; use `git commit --no-verify`. Because of that,
  commit-message validation does not run inside the sandbox — no hook is going
  to catch a malformed message, so get it right yourself before committing.
  This project follows Conventional Commits; see `.claude/rules/git-commit.md`.

A toolchain that can compile and immediately execute code is bounded by
whichever sandbox it runs in — its own if it is declared, execd's own grants if
it is not — and never by argv rules. Ask nono which of the two it is, and what
that sandbox grants, before assuming otherwise.

## Changing the sandbox
Both profiles live in `.agent-sandbox/`, beside the launch scripts in
`.agent-sandbox/bin/`.

- Your own sandbox — this process, its file tools, and any MCP server it spawns
  as a direct child — is `{{.AgentProfilePath}}`.
- The sandbox every shell command runs in is `{{.ProfilePath}}`.

Both are written in nono's own schema; agent-sandbox hands each path to nono.

Editing **does not affect the current session**: the session runs from a copy
of `.agent-sandbox/` the operator approved. The next `agent-sandbox launch`
shows the operator your change and asks before using it. `agent-sandbox doctor`
probes that the hook runs under your profile and asks nono that neither the
binaries nor the approved copy are writable from the sandbox.
